<page>

  <paragraphs>
      <title>Introduction to Limits</title>
<p>
  The main concept that sets Calculus apart from Precalculus is the idea of a limit. Before, if we couldn't plug something into a function, we just gave up. Now, we want to be a little more particular about that.
</p>

<example workspace="5in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the function <md>f(x) = \frac{x^2 - x - 2}{x-2}</md>. Investigate what <m>f</m> looks like near <m>x=2</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution component="a">
    <p>
      
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>
</paragraphs>
</page>
<page>
<example workspace="5in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the function <md>f(x) = \frac{x^2 +4x + 5}{x-1}</md>. Investigate what <m>f</m> looks like near <m>x=1</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution component="a">
    <p>
      
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>
<p>
We want to have a mathematical way to distinguish between these two scenarios, one where it looks like there should be a value and one where there should not be. This is the idea of <q>approaching a value</q> is the idea of a limit.
  </p>
</page>
<page>
  <paragraphs>
    <title>Why care about this?</title>
    <p> It turns out that there are a surprising number of physical problems that revolve around this issue. We'll cover two of them today.  </p>
  </paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>The Tangent Problem</title>
  <example workspace="4in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the equation of the tangent line to the graph of <m>y = x^2</m> at <m>x=2</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="a">
      <p>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <p component="a">
    Effectively, what we needed to do here was plug <m>x=2</m> into <m>\frac{x^2 - 4}{x-2}</m>, and that normally is not allowed. But, by considering nearby points (which we will call <q>taking a limit</q> later), we could get something that looks like it should be the value of this function at the undefined point.
    </p>
</paragraphs>
</page>
<page>
  <paragraphs>
    <title>The Velocity Problem</title>
    <p>
      If you're driving in a car, you have a speedometer that tells you how fast you're going at a particular moment in time. However, if I gave you a function (or graph) of your position as a function of time, how would you get <em>instantaneous velocity</em> from that?
    </p>

    <example workspace="4in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If an object is dropped from a height, physics tells us that the distance the object has fallen is given by <m>s(t) = 4.9t^2</m> where <m>t</m> is in seconds and <m>s</m> is in meters. If a ball is dropped off a skyscraper, how fast is it moving after 3 seconds?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="a">
        <p>
          
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </paragraphs>
</page>
<page>
<p workspace = "1in"> Both of these examples are similar. In each case, we
<ul>
  <li>
    <p workspace = "1in">
      Wanted something that we couldn't directly find
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p workspace = "1in">
      Found an approximation to that by looking at an average rate of change, then
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p workspace = "1in">
      Made the gap smaller and smaller to get to the instantaneous rate of change.
    </p>
  </li>
</ul>
</p>
</page>
<page>
<example workspace = "5in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Find the equation of the tangent line to the graph of <m>y = x^2 + 2x - 3</m> at <m>x=0</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution component="a">
    <p>
      
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>
