  </page>
  <page>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Definition of a Limit</title>
    
      <p>
        Now let's try to give a bit more of a specific definition to this idea we've been hinting at so far. In some sense, we want to talk about the value of a function looks like it's getting closer to as the input approaches a given value. 
      </p>

      <definition xml:id="def-limit-ver1">
      <title>The Limit, attempt 1</title>      
        <statement>
          <p component="b" workspace="2in">
            Let <m>f</m> be a function defined around some point <m>a</m> (but not necessarily at <m>a</m>). We say that <md>
              \lim_{x \rightarrow a} f(x) = L
            </md>, which is read <q>the limit of <m>f(x)</m> as <m>x</m> approaches <m>a</m> is <m>L</m></q>,
          </p>
          <p component="a">
            Let <m>f</m> be a function defined around some point <m>a</m> (but not necessarily at <m>a</m>). We say that <md>
              \lim_{x \rightarrow a} f(x) = L
            </md>, which is read <q>the limit of <m>f(x)</m> as <m>x</m> approaches <m>a</m> is <m>L</m></q>, if <m>f(x)</m> gets closer and closer to <m>L</m> as <m>x</m> gets closer and closer to <m>a</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p component="b" workspace="2in">
        This seems all well and good for all of the examples we've talked about before: <md>
          \lim_{x \rightarrow 2} \frac{x^2 - 4}{x-2} = 4
        </md> because 
        
      </p>
      <p component="a">
        This seems all well and good for all of the examples we've talked about before: <md>
          \lim_{x \rightarrow 2} \frac{x^2 - 4}{x-2} = 4
        </md> because as we plug in values for <m>x</m> that are closer and closer to <m>2</m>, the output gets closer and closer to <m>4</m>.
      </p>
</paragraphs>
</page>
<page>
      <p>
        However, not everything works so well.
      </p>

      <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Consider the statement <md>
              \lim_{x\rightarrow 0} x^2 = -1
            </md>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>

      <p>
        So, maybe that definition wasn't so great. We would like a limit to be a single number, and want the function values to <em>actually</em> get close to the target point, not just <em>closer</em>.
      </p>
</page>
<page>
      <definition xml:id="def-limit-ver2">
        <statement>
          <p component="b" workspace="2in">
            Let <m>f</m> be a function defined around some point <m>a</m> (but not necessarily at <m>a</m>). We say that <md>
              \lim_{x \rightarrow a} f(x) = L
            </md>, which is read <q>the limit of <m>f(x)</m> as <m>x</m> approaches <m>a</m> is <m>L</m></q>,
          </p>
          <p component="a">
            Let <m>f</m> be a function defined around some point <m>a</m> (but not necessarily at <m>a</m>). We say that <md>
              \lim_{x \rightarrow a} f(x) = L
            </md>, which is read <q>the limit of <m>f(x)</m> as <m>x</m> approaches <m>a</m> is <m>L</m></q>, if <m>L</m> is the unique number so that <m>f(x)</m> can be made <em>arbitrarily</em> close to <m>L</m> by picking <m>x</m> <em>sufficiently</em> close to (but not equal to) <m>a</m>, if it exists.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p component="b" workspace="4in">
        Why is this better?
      </p>

      <p component="a">
        This fixes all of the issues from before. 
      </p>

      <p component="b" workspace="5in">
        The other main point is that limits don't have to exist.
      </p>
      <p component="a">
        The definition says <q>if it exists</q>. So there's a possibility for it not to exist. We've seen one of these examples already with a function that had a vertical asymptote, and the values just got really big. 
      </p>
  </page>
  <page>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Graphical Limits</title>
      <p>
        One of the best ways to try to read/interpret limits is from graphs. We already saw how tables can be used to approximate limits; graphs make that process even easier.
      </p>

      <example>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Consider the function <m>f(x)</m> represented by the graph below.
          </p>
          <image width="60%">
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="wksht-limits-graphical-one">
      <xi:include href="../../prefigure/limits-graphical-one.xml" />
      </prefigure>
      </image>
          <p>
            Determine each of the following limits, or state that they don't exist.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow -2} f(x)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 0} f(x)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 1} f(x)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </example>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>
  <page>
    <example>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          For the function <m>f(x)</m> whose graph is drawn below, determine each of the following limits, if they exist.
        </p>
        <image width="60%">
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="wksht-limits-graphical-two">
      <xi:include href="../../prefigure/limits-graphical-two.xml" />
      </prefigure>
      </image>
      </introduction>
      <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow -2} f(x)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 0} f(x)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 1} f(x)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\lim_{x\rightarrow 2} f(x)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
    </example>
  </page>
  <page>
    <paragraphs>
      <title>A special limit</title>
      <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the graph below to approximate the value of <md>\lim_{x\rightarrow 0} \frac{\sin(x)}{x}</md>
          </p>
        <image width="60%">
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="notes-graphical-limits-sinx-x">
      <xi:include href="../../prefigure/limits-graphical-sinx-x.xml" />
      </prefigure>
