  </handout>

    <handout component = "cur">
    <title>Wednesday 9/2</title>
<objectives>
<ul>
<li>
  <p>
    Investigate one-sided limits.
  </p>
  </li>
  <li>
  <p>
    Understand the various ways in which a limit may not exist.
  </p>
  </li>
  </ul>
</objectives>
<page>
<paragraphs>
  <title>Limits that Don't Exist</title>
  
  <p>
    We saw last class a few examples of limits that didn't exist.
  </p>
  <image width="60%">
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="wksht-limits-graphical-one-v2">
      <xi:include href="../../prefigure/limits-graphical-one.xml" />
      </prefigure>
      </image>
  <p>
    However, if I look just from the left or just from the right as I approach the point where the limit doesn't exist, I do actually get a value. Since it may be useful to have or look at these values, we define them.
  </p>
  </paragraphs>
</page>
<page>
  <definition xml:id="def-one-sided-limits">
    <statement>
      <p component="b" workspace="4in">
        Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function defined near a point <m>a</m> (but not necessarily at <m>a</m>). 
      </p>
      <p component="a">
        Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function defined near a point <m>a</m> (but not necessarily at <m>a</m>). We say the <term>left-hand limit</term> of <m>f</m> at <m>a</m> is <m>L</m>, written <md>
          \lim_{x \rightarrow a^-} f(x) = L
        </md>
        if the values of <m>f(x)</m> can be made arbitrarily close to <m>L</m> by picking <m>x</m> sufficiently close to <m>a</m> <em>with <m>x</m> less than <m>a</m></em>.
        </p>
        <p component="a"> 
          We say the <term>right-hand limit</term> of <m>f</m> at <m>a</m> is <m>L</m>, written <md>
          \lim_{x \rightarrow a^+} f(x) = L
        </md>
        if the values of <m>f(x)</m> can be made arbitrarily close to <m>L</m> by picking <m>x</m> sufficiently close to <m>a</m> <em>with <m>x</m> greater than <m>a</m></em>.
      </p>
      <p component="a">
        Together, these are the <term>one-sided limits</term> of <m>f</m> at <m>a</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
</page>
<page>
  <example>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the graph of the function <m>f(x)</m> sketched below.
      </p>
      <image width="60%">
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="wksht-limits-onesided-one">
      <xi:include href="../../prefigure/limits-onesided-one.xml" />
      </prefigure>
      </image>
      <p>
        Determine each of the following or state that they do not exist
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <md>
              \lim_{x\rightarrow -2^-} f(x)
            </md>
          </p>
          <p>
            <md>
              \lim_{x\rightarrow -2^+} f(x)
            </md>
          </p>
          <p>
            <md>
              \lim_{x\rightarrow -2} f(x)
            </md>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <md>
              \lim_{x\rightarrow 0^-} f(x)
            </md>
          </p>
          <p>
            <md>
              \lim_{x\rightarrow 0^+} f(x)
            </md>
          </p>
          <p>
            <md>
              \lim_{x\rightarrow 0} f(x)
            </md>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <sidebyside>
          <p>
            <md>
              \lim_{x\rightarrow 2^-} f(x)
            </md>
          </p>
          <p>
            <md>
              \lim_{x\rightarrow 2^+} f(x)
            </md>
          </p>
          <p>
            <md>
              \lim_{x\rightarrow 2} f(x)
            </md>
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </example>
  </page>
  <page>
    <p>
      Based on our previous example, as well as thinking about how the definitions of limits and one-sided limits relate, we get the following theorem.
    </p>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-one-sided-limits">
      <statement>
        <p component="b" workspace="3in">
          <nbsp />
        </p>
        <p component="a">
          Let <m>f</m> be a function defined near some point <m>a</m>.
          <md>
            \lim_{x\rightarrow a} f(x) = L
          </md>
          if and only if
          <md>
            \lim_{x\rightarrow a^-} f(x) = L \text{ and } \lim_{x\rightarrow a^+} f(x) = L
          </md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
    <p component="a">
      This does require us to know that the one-sided limits exist, but in order to show that a full limit exists, we need to check if the two one-sided limits both exist and are equal.
    </p>
    </page>
    <page>
      <paragraphs>
        <title>Ways for Limits to Not Exist</title>
        <p component="b" workspace="1in">
          We've now seen one way for limits to not exist:
        </p>
        <p component="a">
          We've now seen one way for limits to not exist: the left-hand and right-hand limits don't match.
        </p>
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
          But what else can happen?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Asymptotes, or limits that go to infinity.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Limits that don't exist by oscillation.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </page>
    <page>
      <paragraphs>
        <title>Limits by Oscillation</title>
        <example workspace="4in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Investigate <md>
                \lim_{x\rightarrow 0} \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{x}\right)
              </md>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </example>
      </paragraphs>
    </page>
    <page>
      <paragraphs>
        <title>Limits that go to infinity</title>
        <example workspace="4in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Investigate <md>
                \lim_{x\rightarrow 0} \frac{1}{x^2}
              </md>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </example>
        <p component="b" workspace="3in">
          In order to properly describe what is happening here, we write
          
        </p>
        <p component="a">
          In order to properly describe what is happening here, we write
          <md>
            \lim_{x\rightarrow 0} \frac{1}{x^2} = \infty
          </md>.
          We are not treating <m>\infty</m> like a number. We are not saying that this limit exists and is infinite. We are just using this notation to describe the behavior. If we think of <q>close to infinity</q> as <q>very large</q>, then this fits with our previous definition of a limit.
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </page>
